The river was hidden from sight and the two young men were lost.  One of them managed to climb into a hollow tree and survived the night by eating berries and climbing down to the river at daylight.  The other young man search for him all day but couldn't find him.  He returned to Egulac that evening.  The next day, the young man from the tree went to look for his friend and found him sitting by the fire at the village entrance.  His friend had given up looking for him and was now going to look for him.  They went together to look for food and when they returned to the village, the young man from the tree told his friend about the berries he had eaten.  His friend told him that those were poisonous berries and that he was lucky to be alive.  The young man from Egulac looked at his friend and thought, "Just because you have grown up near the sea and have learned to hunt seals, it does not make you wise."

This story is from the book "Native American Stories" by Campfire Club.  It is an interesting story and shows that even in ancient times, people were aware that there were dangers in the world and that it was important to be careful.


